# Profe Kyle Interactive Reader App

## Read the PRD first
Before starting any work, read docs/PRD.md (the Product Requirements Document).
It contains the full architecture, data structure, feature build order (53 slices across 7 phases), and design principles.
Follow it as your north star. Do not make decisions that contradict the PRD without asking.

## Current Phase
**COMPLETE:** Phase 1, 1.5, 2a (slices 13–22 + 14–15 Stripe webhooks), 2b, 2c (slices 22a–22e writing class), 2.5 (slices 30–32), 3 (slices 33–35), 4 (slices 36–39: knowledge graph, evidence, N+1 routing, `/progress`).
**Next feature build:** Phase 5 (slices 40+). Do not build Phase 5 until asked.

**Session types supported:** `story` (Phase 2a), `writing` (Phase 2c), `exam` (Phase 5, slices 49a–49e — spec complete, not yet built). The exam session type uses group-based submission: one writer per group, Supabase Realtime sync to group members, teacher-led review mode. See PRD Section 4 (GroupExamPrompt, ExamGroup, GroupExamSubmission) and Section 7 (slices 49a–49e).

**Production (learn.profekyle.com):** Site and live Stripe webhook are up (200 on test delivery). Remaining ops: Supabase Auth Site URL + redirect URLs, bulk import of existing ThriveCart/Stripe subscribers, legacy price ID mapping. Teacher invite remains for nicknames and exceptions.

## Tech Stack
- Next.js 14+ (App Router)
- Supabase (PostgreSQL + Auth, magic link)
- Stripe Checkout + StripeCart (payments, webhook auto-account creation)
- TanStack Query (data fetching)
- Vercel (hosting)
- Edge TTS (audio generation, free)
- Bunny Stream (pronunciation video hosting, existing account)

## Local dev
- This app runs at **http://localhost:3004** only (`npm run dev` is pinned to `--port 3004`).
- Verify UI in the browser at that URL. Never probe 3000, 3001, 3002, or 3003 for this repo. Those ports belong to other apps or hung leftover `next dev` processes that hang curl.
- If you need a health check, use `curl --max-time 2 http://127.0.0.1:3004/`. If it is not 200, tell Kyle. Do not start a second Next server and do not scan other ports.

## Rules
1. No em dashes (—) in UI text. Use colons, periods, or commas. Em dashes are an AI telltale. The only em dashes in the app are in story content (—The End—) which is Kyle's existing format.
2. Phonetic transcriptions use IPA everywhere. The app uses IPA for all phonetic displays: tooltips, dictation, choral practice, pronunciation explanations. Kyle's custom symbols (ö, ü, ä, etc.) are deprecated in the app but remain in source story files and teaching materials. One system for the student, not two. New transcriptions use ɑ (not ɔ) for Kyle's open vowel (want, walk, thought). See `src/lib/ipa-conventions.ts`.
3. UI copy is in Spanish for navigation/CTA, English for learning content. "Guardar en mazo" not "Save to deck." The audience is Spanish-speaking.
4. Mobile-first always. If a feature doesn't work on a 375px wide screen, it doesn't ship.
5. Never break a working slice. When adding a new feature, do not modify code from previous working slices unless absolutely necessary. If you must, explain why.
6. Commit after every working slice. Git commit with the slice name and what works. Also push to GitHub (origin/main).
7. Follow the data structure in the PRD. Do not create new tables or fields without updating the PRD first.
8. Kyle's voice in all generated text. Direct, warm, self-deprecating, no jargon, no corporate language. If you generate user-facing text (error messages, feedback, tips), it must sound like Kyle wrote it.
9. Reading is primary. No feature may interrupt or overlay the reading experience. SRS, TPRS, and other tools live on separate pages or behind a clear post-story transition.
10. Contractions everywhere in English content. The app teaches spoken English. Even UI text in English uses contractions.
11. Feedback must be small and actionable. AI feedback identifies no more than two priorities at once, explains them in Kyle's Spanish voice, and offers a next attempt. Never show a giant correction block.
12. No AI overclaiming. The app must never claim an official test score, a CEFR level, a diagnosis, or objective pronunciation perfection. Azure and LLM output is practice guidance.
13. Do not infer what the learner "thought." Store observable behavior. If the reason matters, ask the learner in simple language.
14. Keep the reader calm. Dictation, reverse translation, and pronunciation practice begin after the story or from an explicit learner choice. They never pop up while somebody is reading.
15. Group exam: level-differentiated Task 2. Intermediate = paragraph restructuring (scrambled 7-9 sentence paragraph, letter-coded reordering). Pre-intermediate = sentence correction (7 incorrect + 3 correct sentences, identify and fix errors). The `task2_type` field on GroupExamPrompt auto-selects based on level but can be teacher-overridden. Task 1 (fill-in-the-translation) and Task 3 (translation with conditionals 9-10) are the same for both levels.
16. Group exam: one writer per group. Only the designated writer can submit answers. Other group members see a read-only view with live updates via Supabase Realtime. The ExamGroup entity tracks `writer_id` and `member_ids` for attribution. This is the only session type with group-based (not individual) submission.
17. Read DESIGN.md before any UI work. It defines all color tokens, spacing, typography, components, navigation architecture, and page layouts. The app uses a Paper Light theme (warm off-white background, terracotta + moss palette). NOT dark mode. When creating or modifying any UI, read and follow DESIGN.md first.

## UI Design North Stars
- Read and follow DESIGN.md at the project root BEFORE writing or modifying any UI. It is the source of truth for all colors, fonts, spacing, components, navigation, and page layouts. Do not contradict it.
- The app uses a "Paper Light" theme (warm off-white #faf6f0 background, earthy terracotta + moss palette). NOT dark mode. The previous instruction to "build dark-first" is obsolete and wrong.
- One layout, not two. The app is a mobile app that breathes on desktop (centered, max-width 672px). No separate desktop layout. No sidebars, no multi-column dashboards, no split views. See DESIGN.md "Desktop Adaptations" section.
- Mobile-first always. Test at 375px width. 500 million LatAm users already know bottom-tab and card-list patterns. When a student opens the app for the first time, they should know where to tap without instructions.
- Calm, like reading a book. No gamification, no flashy animations, no emojis as icons. See DESIGN.md "Do's and Don'ts" section.

## Frontend Design Standards
These rules are a quick reference. DESIGN.md is the full spec. When in doubt, read DESIGN.md.

### Visual System
- All color tokens, typography, spacing, border radius, and elevation rules are defined in DESIGN.md. Read it before touching any UI.
- Color tokens are CSS variables in globals.css, mapped to Tailwind classes via the @theme block. Use `bg-paper`, `text-text-primary`, `border-paper-line`, `bg-accent`, etc. Never scatter hex values across components. Never use Material Design 3 token names.
- Theme is Paper Light (warm off-white). NOT dark mode. NOT pure white for reading areas. Use `--paper` (#faf6f0).
- Two font families: Lora (headlines + story text, serif) and Roboto Flex (UI labels, sans-serif). Self-host via next/font with font-display: swap. See DESIGN.md for the full type scale.
- Spacing: 4px base scale (4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 32, 40, 48, 64). No arbitrary values.
- Border radius: 8px (small), 16px (cards/buttons/inputs), 24px (modals/sheets), 9999px (step nav pills + circular elements only). See DESIGN.md for the pill vs. rounded rectangle rule.
- Elevation: tonal layering (white on paper + 1px borders). No shadows on cards. Sticky elements (audio player, header) may use a subtle shadow. See DESIGN.md.

### Component Consistency
- Buttons: primary (filled terracotta), secondary (outlined), ghost (text/icon), and step-nav (pill, step navigation only). Defined in DESIGN.md.
- Inputs: shared styling. Focus state thickens border to 2px terracotta. See DESIGN.md.
- Cards: white surface, 1px paper-line border, 24px radius, no shadow. See DESIGN.md.
- Icons: Lucide React only. Never emojis. See DESIGN.md icon mapping table.
- Every interactive element must have a clear hover state (desktop) and active/pressed state (mobile).

### UX States (non-negotiable)
Every feature that fetches data or performs an action must handle ALL of these states. See DESIGN.md "Loading, Empty, and Error States" section for full specs.
- **Loading state:** skeleton loaders (gray pulse shapes matching content layout). Never blank screens.
- **Error state:** friendly message in Kyle's voice ("Algo salio mal. Intenta de nuevo.") with retry button. Never raw error codes.
- **Empty state:** helpful message in Spanish, Kyle's voice, with Lucide icon. Never blank pages.
- **Success state:** brief visual confirmation before transitioning.

### Touch and Mobile
- Minimum touch target: 44x44px for all interactive elements. 48x48px for primary actions. See DESIGN.md.
- No hover-dependent functionality on mobile.
- Prevent layout shift: reserve space for dynamic content (audio player, tooltips, modals).
- Test at 375px width before considering anything done.

### Accessibility
- Color contrast: WCAG AA (4.5:1 normal, 3:1 large) against the paper background.
- Keyboard navigation: all interactive elements reachable via Tab/Enter/Escape. Modals trap focus.
- Screen reader support: semantic HTML, aria-label where needed, aria-hidden on decorative elements.
- Never rely on color alone. Green also has icon/text. Red also has explanation.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion: disable animations for users with this setting.

### Animations and Transitions
- 200ms transitions. Fade + slight slide for step transitions. No bounce, no rotation, no scale.
- No flashy animations. No spinning (except loading skeletons). The app feels calm, like reading a book.
- The audio progress bar and karaoke highlight are the only elements that move continuously.

### Navigation Architecture
- Two modes that never overlap: Browsing mode (bottom tab bar: Inicio, Lecciones, Herramientas) and Lesson mode (header with back button + lesson-specific subheader, no tab bar). See DESIGN.md "Navigation Architecture" section.
- Tab bar and sticky audio player never coexist (different modes).
- New lesson types plug into Lesson mode without architecture changes.
- See DESIGN.md "Page Layouts" section for every page's section order, spacing, and focal point.

## Security Standards
These rules apply to all backend code, API routes, database access, and authentication flows. Security is not optional, even for an early-stage product with "non-sensitive" data.

### Data Access (Supabase Row Level Security)
- Enable RLS on EVERY table in Supabase. No exceptions. Without RLS, any client with the publishable (anon) key can read and write all data.
- RLS policies for student tables: a student can only SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE rows where `user_id = auth.uid()`. They can never read another student's data.
- RLS policies for teacher tables: a teacher can only SELECT/UPDATE rows for courses where `teacher_id = auth.uid()`. They can never see another teacher's courses or students.
- RLS policies for classroom data: comprehension responses, personal responses, dictation attempts, word lookups, attendance records are all scoped to the authenticated user. A classroom student sees only their own responses. A teacher sees responses for students enrolled in their courses.
- The teacher dashboard queries must verify that the requesting user is the teacher of the course before returning student data. Never trust a client-side role check alone.
- Use Supabase service role key (secret key) ONLY in server-side API routes and scripts, never in client-side code. The publishable (anon) key is for the browser.

### API Route Protection
- Every API route must verify authentication before processing. Check the Supabase session token. If no valid session, return 401.
- API routes that return student data must verify the requester is authorized to see that data (student sees own data, teacher sees their students' data).
- Never accept `user_id` from the client request body. Always derive the user from the authenticated session token. A client should never be able to say "I'm user X" — the server knows who they are from the token.
- Stripe webhook routes must verify the Stripe signature header before processing. Never process a webhook without verifying it came from Stripe.

### Input Validation
- Validate and sanitize ALL user input on the server side, never trust the client. This includes: comprehension answers, personal question responses, dictation attempts, display names, course names, session notes.
- Use a validation library (Zod is recommended, works well with TypeScript and Next.js). Define schemas for every API input.
- Limit input length: text fields have a max character count (e.g., comprehension answers max 500 chars, personal responses max 1000 chars, display names max 50 chars). Reject inputs that exceed limits.
- Strip HTML tags from user input to prevent XSS. Never render user input as HTML without sanitization. If using `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` (which the reader does for word spans), ensure the HTML is generated server-side from trusted data, not from user input.

### Rate Limiting and Abuse Prevention
- AI feedback API route (`/api/check-answer`): rate limit to max 10 requests per user per hour. This prevents abuse and controls API costs. Return a friendly "Has intentado muchas veces. Intenta de nuevo más tarde." message when the limit is hit.
- Stripe webhook route: idempotency. If the same webhook event is received twice (Stripe retries), process it only once. Store processed event IDs and skip duplicates.
- Session link tokens: validate that the token exists, is not expired, and belongs to an active session. Rate limit token validation attempts to prevent brute-force guessing.

### Environment Variables
- NEVER expose server-side keys to the client. The following must ONLY be in server-side code or Vercel environment variables (not prefixed with NEXT_PUBLIC_): `SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY` (or service role key), `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET`, `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY`.
- Only `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL` and `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` (publishable key) are safe for the browser.
- Never commit `.env.local` or any file containing secrets to Git. The `.gitignore` must include `.env*`.
- Never log secrets or API keys to the console, even during debugging.

### Session and Authentication
- Magic link tokens are single-use and expire. Supabase handles this, but verify the session is still valid on each protected route.
- Session link tokens (for Zoom chat enrollment) must be: unique, unguessable (use crypto.randomUUID or a 32+ character random string), and tied to a specific session. They should not be guessable from the session ID or story slug.
- When a student clicks a session link, verify: (1) the token is valid and belongs to an active session, (2) the session hasn't been deleted, (3) the student's subscription is active (for material access). If any check fails, show a clear error.
- Logout should clear the session completely. No lingering auth state.

### Data Privacy
- Student data (responses, lookups, attendance) is visible only to: the student themselves, the teacher of their course, and Kyle (as admin). No one else.
- Never expose student emails or personal information in client-side code, API responses to other students, or URLs.
- If the app is ever sold to other teachers, each teacher's data must be fully isolated. A teacher from School A cannot see students or data from School B. This is enforced at the database level via RLS, not just in the UI.
- Consider GDPR/LatAm data protection implications: students should be able to request deletion of their data. Build a "delete my data" path early (even if it's a manual process Kyle handles for now).

## Code Quality and Architecture
- Follow web app design best practices at all times.
- Structure the codebase to be future-proof, well-organized, flexible, and easy to extend.
- Prefer composition over inheritance. Extract reusable logic into hooks or utility functions.
- Keep components small and focused. One component = one responsibility.
- Use TypeScript properly: define types for all data structures, avoid `any`, use the PRD's data structure definitions as the source of truth for types.
- Name variables and functions descriptively. Code should be self-documenting.
- Do not hardcode values that should be configurable (story IDs, API URLs, audio paths). Use constants, env vars, or config files.
- Think ahead: every component you build should be designed so that adding the next feature (the next slice in the PRD) does not require rewriting what you already built. If a component is tightly coupled to one specific story or one specific use case, refactor it to be generic.
- Separation of concerns: data fetching, business logic, and presentation should be in separate layers.
- Follow Next.js App Router conventions. Server components by default, client components only when interactivity is needed (and marked with "use client").
- Keep the file structure organized: components in `src/components/`, API routes in `src/app/api/`, types in `src/types/`, utilities in `src/lib/`.

## Build discipline
- Build one vertical slice at a time (see PRD Section 7 for the slice list)
- Each slice must be fully working and verified on a real device before the next begins
- Git commit AND push to GitHub after every working slice
- Do not skip ahead to later phases
- If something breaks, revert to the last working commit and fix from there

## Existing code assets
- PronunciationPro2 at ~/Desktop/webdev/PronunciationPro2/ contains a working Azure Speech pronunciation assessment pipeline with LatAm-specific coaching rules. The server code (TypeScript/Express) and client (React) are reference implementations for Phase 3. Do not rebuild this from scratch when we reach Phase 3.
- The project is at ~/Desktop/webdev/interactive-reader/. GitHub repo: github.com/SwiftSwede/interactive-reader (public). After every commit, push to origin/main.

## Key decisions (resolved 2026-08-21)
- Teacher-first build strategy: Phase 2 = full teacher dashboard. Consumer paid tier = Phase 5.
- Same reader, different overlay: Classroom and consumer students use identical components. Context determines active features.
- Magic link auth: no passwords. Email -> magic link -> click -> in.
- Stripe webhook auto-creates user accounts from payment email.
- 90-min class window: reveal gated during class, auto-unlocks at 90 min, manual override available.
- Choral practice + dictation: consumer only. Classroom students do choral practice live with Kyle on Zoom.
- Personal questions: oral in class ("Discutir en clase" label), text input + AI in review mode.
- No due dates: Kyle's methodology: learn IN class, not outside.
- IPA everywhere in the app.
- Group exam (Phase 5, spec complete): collaborative, not individual. One writer per group, real-time sync to members. No grades. Teacher reveals answers question by question in review mode. Level-differentiated Task 2 (intermediate: paragraph restructuring; pre-intermediate: sentence correction). See PRD slices 49a-49e and Language-Wiki/concepts/group-exam-methodology.md.
